INT4 prefill chunks: dequantize the cached K/V per layer, run FlashAttention-2.

The packed int4 kernel in v1/attention/ops/int4_per_token_head.py runs
prefill chunks in its 2D mode: one query block of BLOCK_Q tokens per CTA,
each streaming and unpacking the whole KV range of the request. At 100k
context and 1,024 query tokens that is 2.5 TFLOP against 1.7 GB of KV per
layer, compute-bound, and the kernel sits at a third of the HMMA rate FA2
reaches on the same work (56.5 ms per layer against FA2's 30.1 with the
tensor-core patch; 110 ms before it).

This patch adds a second route for exactly those batches: for every request,
a Triton kernel writes the request's cached K and V (all seqused_k tokens,
the current chunk included) into two contiguous bf16 buffers, undoing the
writer per (token, head): nibbles to values, minus the 4-bit zero point in
the scale's low mantissa bits, times the scale, even/odd lanes interleaved
back to head order. The values stay rotated, as stored; Q is rotated and
the output un-rotated by the existing entry point. Then FA2 varlen runs on
the buffers with the request's own cu_seqlens_k, causal aligned to the last
positions like the packed kernel. The route is taken only when every request
of the batch is longer than the 3D cap (max_query_len_3d, default 16) and
none of softcap, sinks, alibi, qq_bias, output_scale, mm_prefix_range or
per-sequence causal flags is set; everything else, decode and verify batches
in particular, is unchanged and never reaches the .tolist() sync.

Buffers: 2 x alloc_len x num_kv_heads x head_size in the query dtype, where
alloc_len is the block table's capacity; 1.07 GB at 262,144 max-model-len.
They come from the workspace manager, reserved on the first call of the
entry point (the profile run) while it is unlocked, so the pool accounts for
them; without a manager, or if it is locked, they are plain allocations.

Measured on an RTX 4080 SUPER, 100k context, one layer, real writer:

  query tokens   FA2 bf16   packed kernel   this route   rel. RMS vs packed
  1024           30.1 ms    56.8 ms         29.3 ms      0.0089
  2048           59.3 ms    102.4 ms        57.1 ms      0.0089

The 0.0089 is the packed kernel's own int8 Q rounding (0.0082 against an
fp32 reference); against the bf16 FA2 reference both are at 0.174, the
quantization noise of the cache on random data.
In the server (torch profiler, 100k prompt, DFlash2 k=7, per layer):

  prefill chunk, 1,698 tokens   packed kernel 85.5 ms   dequant 0.8 ms + FA2 49.0 ms
  prefill chunk,   432 tokens   packed kernel 28.4 ms   dequant 0.8 ms + FA2 16.2 ms
  verify, grid [1,4,16]         0.690 ms before and after (route not taken)

bench/labd_bench.py, 100k context, same session:

  first prefill of the 100k prompt   before 96 s    after 79 s    (bf16 arm 77 s)
  TTFT on the cached prompt          before 3.2 s   after 2.45 s  (bf16 arm 0.8 s; the
                                     remainder is the hybrid block rule: 2,126 of the
                                     tokens are recomputed at B = 1696)
  decode                             unchanged, copy 41.3 ms/step, 66.2 tok/s

KV pool at 262,144 max-model-len: 615,931 -> 564,071 tokens (the buffers).

